<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/meeting.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/meeting-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/meeting.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/appraisal-solutions/">Services</a><span>›</span>Umpire Services &amp; Selection</nav><span class="kicker light up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Insurance Appraisal Umpire Services in Wisconsin</h1><p class="lede up" style="animation-delay:.14s">When two appraisers can’t settle every item, the umpire decides what’s left. A good umpire is fair, technically sharp and completely independent of both sides.</p><div class="btn-row up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Role</small><b>Neutral decision-maker</b></div><div><small>Chosen by</small><b>Both appraisers or a court</b></div><div><small>Decides</small><b>Disputed amounts only</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Insurance appraisal umpire services in Wisconsin cover two related needs: serving as the neutral umpire on an appraisal panel, and helping the two appraisers identify and agree on a qualified umpire. When we serve as umpire, we represent neither the policyholder nor the insurer.</p>
<h2>What an umpire does</h2>
<p>Under a typical appraisal clause, each side names an appraiser and the two appraisers select an umpire. The appraisers try to agree on the amount of loss. Items they settle are set. On items where they differ, the umpire reviews the evidence and signs with one appraiser or the other, or with both on a figure in between. Agreement in writing by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p>
<h3>What an umpire does not do</h3>
<p>An umpire does not decide coverage, interpret exclusions or rule on how a claim was handled. Questions of bad faith or late payment belong in a different forum. The umpire’s job is limited to valuing the disputed items.</p>
<h2>Serving as umpire</h2>
<p>When appointed, we disclose any prior dealings with either appraiser, the insurer or the policyholder before accepting. We then:</p>
<ol>
<li>Review both appraisers’ estimates, photos and supporting documents.</li>
<li>Inspect the property, usually with both appraisers present.</li>
<li>Ask each appraiser to explain the remaining disputed items.</li>
<li>Request additional information, such as engineering reports or test results, where needed.</li>
<li>Issue a decision item by item so the award is clear and traceable.</li>
</ol>
<p>We keep communication transparent. Neither appraiser should hear something about the dispute that the other doesn’t.</p>
<h2>Helping panels choose an umpire</h2>
<p>Panels sometimes stall at the umpire step. One side proposes names, the other objects, and weeks pass. We can help by suggesting qualified candidates with relevant experience, such as commercial roofing or fire restoration, and by laying out how each candidate approaches the role. The final choice belongs to the two appraisers.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">If appraisers can’t agree</b>Most policies say that if the appraisers can’t agree on an umpire within a set time, either party may ask a court to appoint one. In Wisconsin that request typically goes to the circuit court in the county where the property is located, though policy language varies. Consult a Wisconsin attorney about that step.</div>
<h2>What makes a good umpire</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Independence.</strong> No financial interest in the outcome and no ongoing relationship that would create doubt.</li>
<li><strong>Technical knowledge.</strong> Enough construction and estimating experience to evaluate competing estimates on their merits.</li>
<li><strong>Clear reasoning.</strong> Decisions explained so both sides understand the result, even when they disagree.</li>
<li><strong>Responsiveness.</strong> Prompt scheduling and a decision without unnecessary delay.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Learn more</h2>
<p>Our <a href="/appraisal-process-guide/">appraisal process guide</a> explains where the umpire fits in the sequence, and the <a href="/insurance-appraisal-faq/">appraisal FAQ</a> covers common questions about costs and timing. Appraisers looking for an umpire can <a href="/contact-us/">contact us</a> with the claim details and the parties involved so we can check for conflicts.</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays the umpire?</summary><div class="a"><p>Most policies split the umpire’s fee equally between the policyholder and the insurer. Check your policy’s appraisal clause for the exact terms.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you serve as umpire if you’ve worked with one of the appraisers before?</summary><div class="a"><p>We disclose any prior relationships before accepting. Both appraisers then decide whether they are comfortable proceeding.</p></div></details><details><summary>How is an umpire appointed if the appraisers deadlock?</summary><div class="a"><p>Most policies allow either side to ask a court to appoint one. In Wisconsin that is typically the circuit court for the county where the property sits, but read your policy and consult an attorney.</p></div></details></div>
